BIOL1550.description:
Protozoan parasites influence the health of human populations in less developed countries, leading to severe human suffering and loss of economic development. Understanding the fundamental biology of these pathogens and how they are transmitted is essential for developing treatments and mitigation strategies to improve human health. Focuses on biochemical, genetic, cellular, and immunological aspects of parasite and host responses. Expected: <a href="/search/?p=BIOL%200470" data-action="result-detail" data-group="code:BIOL 0470" class="notoffered" title="This course is not being offered in the selected term.  Please try another term or search in the University Bulletin for course details.">BIOL 0470</a> or <a href="/search/?p=BIOL%200530" data-action="result-detail" data-group="code:BIOL 0530" class="notoffered" title="This course is not being offered in the selected term.  Please try another term or search in the University Bulletin for course details.">BIOL 0530</a>.<br/>
<br/>
This course was formerly &quot;Biology of Emerging Microbial Diseases&quot;

CPSY1591.description:
How does vision provide the brain with information about the three-dimensional structure of the world? What types of information are essential for complex organisms like human primates to interact effectively with their environment?
In this course, students will explore these fundamental questions by conducting original research using state-of-the-art visualization technologies. They will:
(1) develop scientifically grounded hypotheses;
(2) design and implement rigorous empirical tests;
(3) construct experimental setups in a lab equipped with advanced virtual reality systems; and
(4) collect and analyze behavioral data to produce a comprehensive scientific report.
